WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.192
The Model Context Protocol, or MCP, is a standard way to connect a language model application to tools and data.

2
00:00:08.392 --> 00:00:14.930
There are three roles: the host application, the client inside it, and the server that exposes capabilities.

3
00:00:15.130 --> 00:00:21.284
One client talks to exactly one server, but a host can run many clients at once.

4
00:00:21.484 --> 00:00:25.715
Servers can offer three kinds of things: tools, resources, and prompts.

5
00:00:25.915 --> 00:00:31.300
Tools are functions the model can decide to call, like searching a ticket system.

6
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:38.423
Resources are read-only data the application can attach as context, such as a file or a database row.

7
00:00:38.623 --> 00:00:44.008
Prompts are reusable templates that a user picks explicitly, often shown as slash commands.

8
00:00:44.208 --> 00:00:45.746
Messages use JSON-RPC 2.0.

9
00:00:45.946 --> 00:00:52.484
For transport, local servers usually use standard input and output, so the host just launches a subprocess.

10
00:00:52.684 --> 00:00:57.299
Remote servers use streamable HTTP, which replaced the older server-sent events transport.

11
00:00:57.499 --> 00:01:03.653
Every session starts with an initialize request where both sides declare their protocol version and capabilities.

12
00:01:03.853 --> 00:01:08.468
After that, the client calls tools/list to discover what the server offers.

13
00:01:08.668 --> 00:01:14.437
Each tool comes with a name, a description, and a JSON schema for its input.

14
00:01:14.637 --> 00:01:21.175
The description matters a lot because the model reads it to decide when to use the tool.

15
00:01:21.375 --> 00:01:27.913
I keep tool descriptions under about two sentences and say exactly when not to use the tool.

16
00:01:28.113 --> 00:01:33.113
When the model wants to act, the client sends tools/call with the arguments.

17
00:01:33.313 --> 00:01:39.082
The server returns content blocks, usually text, and can flag the result as an error.

18
00:01:39.282 --> 00:01:45.436
Returning an error result instead of throwing lets the model see the message and try again.

19
00:01:45.636 --> 00:01:49.098
For remote servers, authorization is based on OAuth 2.1.

20
00:01:49.298 --> 00:01:56.221
A big risk is a tool description that contains hidden instructions, which is a form of prompt injection.

21
00:01:56.421 --> 00:02:01.806
Only install servers you trust, and review what each tool is allowed to touch.

22
00:02:02.006 --> 00:02:08.160
For testing, the MCP Inspector lets you call tools by hand and see the raw messages.

23
00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:12.975
You run it with npx and point it at your server command.

24
00:02:13.175 --> 00:02:22.790
In my own setup I run four servers: git, a filesystem server limited to one folder, a browser, and a Postgres server in read-only mode.

25
00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:29.528
Start with one server that solves a real problem, then add more once you trust the workflow.
